model {
  extend basalt.productionOps {
    metadata {
      constraint "Cloud-platform family adoption moves one organization's accounts from the provider ref an app lineage retained across additive scope changes onto the cloud-platform connector family's canonical provider, which the host derives from its shared declaration. The request names the target ref the consuming apps use, and it must equal that identity. Only a ref whose stored definition derives that identity moves. The exact reviewed account set and generations must match, and the dry run lists the versions and apps that move; a pending sign-in, an acquisition outside the selection or a provider-keyed OAuth client refuses. The dry run and the GO's own inspection also refuse while the organization's release catalog sweep is queued or running, or while an app whose active version names the retained ref has a deploy reserved that has not activated; a failed deploy keeps reading that way until the app's next successful deploy. App deploys choose provider refs again under their final app lock from the version the build continued from, as it is then, so a deploy that read that version before the adoption rewrote it in place writes the family ref after it. Account IDs, credentials, generations and links stay; account, version, connection and acquisition provider refs change in one receipt, and every kept link must resolve to the family provider afterwards. After the GO, and again after the first later deploy, a dry run with no accounts must plan zero changes. The dry run names the apps already on the family ref that start offering the moved accounts, and a test pins the family ref so a declaration change ships with its own adoption. Provider rows are only read."
    }
  }
}
